    "message": {
     "content": "Here is a detailed breakdown of where this offer stands, how Google\u2019s equity structure really works, and an exact playbook for negotiating from your high-leverage position.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is this a good offer?\n\n**Verdict:** It is a **solid baseline offer, but underwhelming for poaching a happy Meta IC6.** You should **negotiate**. \n\nBecause Google reached out to you and you are content at Meta, you hold almost all the leverage. Accepting this offer as-is leaves significant money on the table.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Breakdown: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6 Offer\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Offered (Google L6) | Delta / Analysis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Google offered below your current base) |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annualized Equity** | ~$250,000/yr | ~$270,000/yr ($1.08M / 4) | **+$20,000/yr** (flat average) |\n| **Sign-on Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (typical range for L6 poaches is $50k\u2013$100k+) |\n| **Headline Annual TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+5% (Nominal gain)** |\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Nuance You Need to Know: Google\u2019s Front-Loaded Equity\n\nGoogle typically vests initial grants on a **front-loaded schedule** (usually **33% / 33% / 22% / 12%** or **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** over 4 years).\n\nAssuming the standard **33/33/22/12** schedule on a $1.08M grant:\n* **Year 1:** $235k (Base) + $47k (Bonus) + $360k (33.3% RSU) = **~$642,000**\n* **Year 2:** $235k (Base) + $47k (Bonus) + $360k (33.3% RSU) = **~$642,000**\n* **Year 3:** $235k (Base) + $47k (Bonus) + $240k (22.2% RSU) = **~$522,000** *(drops below your current Meta comp without refreshes)*\n* **Year 4:** $235k (Base) + $47k (Bonus) + $120k (11.1% RSU) = **~$402,000**\n\n#### Why the recruiter emphasized refreshes:\nGoogle relies on annual refresh grants (which vest 25% per year over 4 years) to prevent your comp from dropping off in Years 3 and 4. However, refreshes are strictly tied to performance ratings (GRAD). You should **not** rely on future uncommitted refreshes to justify taking an offer that dips below your baseline later on.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What and How Much to Negotiate\n\nDo not walk away yet\u2014Google expects an L6 candidate from Meta to negotiate. Target the following counters:\n\n#### A. Base Salary: Counter with $255,000 \u2013 $260,000\n* **Reasoning:** Google offered you $235k, which is a pay cut on base salary and near the midpoint of their $183k\u2013$271k posted range. Asking for ~$255k is well within their band and ensures your guaranteed cash increases.\n\n#### B. Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000\n* **Reasoning:** Google gave you $0 sign-on. Because you are walking away from an established team, known performance track record, and upcoming vests at Meta, an upfront transition bonus is standard practice for senior external hires.\n\n#### C. Equity (GSUs): Counter with $1,250,000 \u2013 $1,350,000 over 4 years\n* **Reasoning:** A $1.28M grant yields ~$425k/yr in Years 1\u20132 (front-loaded) and ~$320k/yr flat average. This brings your annualized run-rate closer to **$620k\u2013$650k/yr**, which provides a legitimate financial incentive to switch.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Strategy & Script\n\nBecause you are happy where you are, your tone should be warm, collaborative, but firm on value.\n\n**Talking points for your recruiter call:**\n\n> *\"I've really enjoyed the conversations with the team, and the scope of the L6 role is exciting. However, as I mentioned early on, I'm in a great spot at Meta with strong momentum, and I'm currently tracking around $526k in total compensation with a $240k base.\"*\n>\n> *\"Looking at the numbers, the initial offer gives me a lower base salary and only a modest bump in annualized compensation. When factoring in the risk of leaving my current unvested equity and performance track record, the delta doesn't quite make sense for me to move.\"*\n>\n> *\"If we can get the package closer to the following, I would be thrilled to sign immediately:*\n> * * **Base Salary:** $255,000 (to reflect my current base and band level)*\n> * * **Sign-on Bonus:** $80,000 (to bridge the transition risk)*\n> * * **Equity:** $1.28M over 4 years*\n>\n> *Is this something you can take back to the compensation committee?\"*\n\n### Summary\n* **Do not accept as-is:** The guaranteed base is lower, and the 4-year average is only marginally better.\n* **Do not walk away yet:** Push back firmly using the script above. Google frequently has room on equity and sign-on bonuses for strong L6 hires coming out of Meta.",
